1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:07,690
>> Picture this: we fire tiny particles, one at a time, at a wall with two narrow slits in it.

2
00:00:07,770 --> 00:00:12,880
>> And each one that gets through lands on the screen behind as a single dot.

3
00:00:12,960 --> 00:00:16,400
>> At first, the dots seem to land almost anywhere.

4
00:00:16,480 --> 00:00:18,270
>> Just random, then?

5
00:00:18,350 --> 00:00:22,220
>> Keep going, though, and they sort themselves into stripes.

6
00:00:22,300 --> 00:00:25,890
>> Stripes like these are what waves make when they overlap.

7
00:00:25,970 --> 00:00:27,980
>> But we sent particles.

8
00:00:28,060 --> 00:00:32,500
>> So what actually went through the slits: a particle, or a wave?

9
00:00:32,580 --> 00:00:37,800
>> That's the puzzle at the centre of the double-slit experiment.

10
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:46,090
>> People argued for centuries about what light actually is.

11
00:00:46,170 --> 00:00:52,780
>> In his Treatise on Light of 1690, Christiaan Huygens described light as a wave.

12
00:00:52,860 --> 00:00:58,250
>> And Isaac Newton's Opticks, from 1704, leaned toward particles.

13
00:00:58,330 --> 00:01:06,000
>> In later editions he asked: "Are not the Rays of Light very small Bodies emitted from shining Substances?"

14
00:01:06,080 --> 00:01:09,620
>> Tiny bodies, flying out of anything that shines.

15
00:01:09,700 --> 00:01:17,740
>> And Newton's reputation was so great that, through the 18th century, the particle view held a wide lead.

16
00:01:17,820 --> 00:01:21,510
>> So how would you even tell a wave from a stream of particles?

17
00:01:21,590 --> 00:01:28,480
>> Send particles through two slits, and you'd expect two bright bands, one behind each slit.

18
00:01:28,560 --> 00:01:34,680
But waves can do something particles can't: when two meet, they can add up, or cancel out.

19
00:01:34,760 --> 00:01:38,150
>> Cancel out? Two waves can add up to nothing?

20
00:01:38,230 --> 00:01:44,920
>> Crest on crest makes a bigger wave; crest on trough makes nothing at all. That's interference.

21
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:51,020
>> And particles, as far as anyone could see, shouldn't be able to do that.

22
00:01:53,620 --> 00:02:00,310
Enter Thomas Young: a British doctor and polymath, who later helped decipher the Rosetta Stone.

23
00:02:00,390 --> 00:02:04,210
>> Light waves and the Rosetta Stone. Quite a range.

24
00:02:04,290 --> 00:02:12,430
>> On 24 November 1803, he described an experiment anyone could repeat "whenever the sun shines".

25
00:02:12,510 --> 00:02:19,200
>> He let sunlight in through a small hole, and held a card a thirtieth of an inch wide in the beam.

26
00:02:19,280 --> 00:02:24,090
>> In the card's shadow appeared coloured fringes: stripes of light and dark.

27
00:02:24,170 --> 00:02:27,640
>> In the shadow, where you'd expect plain darkness.

28
00:02:27,720 --> 00:02:32,910
>> And when he blocked the light on one side of the card, the fringes vanished.

29
00:02:32,990 --> 00:02:37,900
>> So the fringes needed light from both sides, meeting and interfering.

30
00:02:37,980 --> 00:02:42,800
>> So it's really a two-path experiment, just with a card instead of slits.

31
00:02:42,880 --> 00:02:50,090
>> And his lectures, published in 1807, describe the version we know: light through two narrow slits.

32
00:02:50,170 --> 00:02:53,930
>> Though whether he ever actually did it with two slits isn't clear.

33
00:02:54,010 --> 00:02:58,620
>> So the famous version may have been partly a thought experiment.

34
00:02:58,700 --> 00:03:06,720
>> Either way, the turn came in 1818, when Augustin-Jean Fresnel entered a French prize contest with a wave theory.

35
00:03:06,800 --> 00:03:12,240
>> One judge, Siméon Poisson, spotted what looked like an absurd prediction.

36
00:03:12,320 --> 00:03:13,660
>> What was it?

37
00:03:13,740 --> 00:03:19,580
>> In the middle of a small round disc's shadow, the theory said, there should be a bright spot.

38
00:03:19,660 --> 00:03:24,530
>> So the head of the judges, François Arago, tried it, and there was the spot.

39
00:03:24,610 --> 00:03:27,250
>> The absurd prediction came true.

40
00:03:27,330 --> 00:03:31,590
>> After that, most scientists accepted that light is a wave.

41
00:03:31,670 --> 00:03:40,910
>> And in 1865, James Clerk Maxwell predicted it's an electromagnetic wave, so the particle idea seemed finished.

42
00:03:40,990 --> 00:03:43,430
>> Seemed.

43
00:03:46,030 --> 00:03:50,970
>> So how do two slits make a whole row of stripes, not just two bands?

44
00:03:51,050 --> 00:03:56,390
Each slit acts as a new source, sending out waves that spread in circles.

45
00:03:56,470 --> 00:04:02,130
>> And to reach a point on the screen, one wave usually travels a little farther than the other.

46
00:04:02,210 --> 00:04:09,350
>> If that extra distance is a whole number of wavelengths, crests arrive together, and the spot is bright.

47
00:04:09,430 --> 00:04:14,170
>> And if it's half a wavelength, crest meets trough, and the spot is dark.

48
00:04:14,250 --> 00:04:20,840
>> Slide along the screen and the extra distance grows steadily: bright, dark, bright, dark.

49
00:04:20,920 --> 00:04:24,790
>> So the spacing of the stripes should follow from the geometry.

50
00:04:24,870 --> 00:04:32,080
>> It does: it's the wavelength, times the distance to the screen, divided by the gap between the slits.

51
00:04:32,160 --> 00:04:40,270
>> Take slits half a millimetre apart, light with a wavelength of 0.6 micrometres, and a screen 1 metre away.

52
00:04:40,350 --> 00:04:44,970
>> Then the stripes come out 1.2 millimetres apart.

53
00:04:45,050 --> 00:04:49,760
Move the screen twice as far away, and they spread twice as wide.

54
00:04:49,840 --> 00:04:52,430
>> And if the slits move closer together?

55
00:04:52,510 --> 00:05:00,950
>> They spread out too: Young described stripes "wider also in the same proportion as the apertures are closer to each other."

56
00:05:01,030 --> 00:05:08,700
>> And it works backwards: Young used fringes like these to estimate the wavelengths of colours, close to modern values.

57
00:05:08,780 --> 00:05:14,400
>> Measuring a wave far too small to see, just from the stripes.

58
00:05:17,000 --> 00:05:19,570
>> But the wave picture had a crack in it.

59
00:05:19,650 --> 00:05:27,160
In 1905, Albert Einstein proposed that light's energy comes in separate lumps, now called photons.

60
00:05:27,240 --> 00:05:30,350
>> Lumps of light? After Young and Maxwell?

61
00:05:30,430 --> 00:05:35,570
>> It explained the photoelectric effect, and later won him a Nobel Prize.

62
00:05:35,650 --> 00:05:42,790
>> Which raises a question: what happens to the stripes when light is so dim that it arrives a little at a time?

63
00:05:42,870 --> 00:05:48,660
>> In 1909, a young Cambridge physicist, Geoffrey Ingram Taylor, tried it.

64
00:05:48,740 --> 00:05:54,130
He dimmed a gas light with dark glass, until it was like a candle more than a mile away.

65
00:05:54,210 --> 00:05:57,550
>> A candle more than a mile off? That's faint.

66
00:05:57,630 --> 00:06:03,570
>> So faint that his photographic plate, behind a sewing needle, took three months to expose.

67
00:06:03,650 --> 00:06:06,020
>> And the fringes were still there.

68
00:06:06,100 --> 00:06:11,520
>> Paul Dirac later put it this way: "each photon then interferes only with itself."

69
00:06:11,600 --> 00:06:15,670
>> Interferes with itself. That's a strange sentence.

70
00:06:15,750 --> 00:06:24,340
>> It is, and here's a caution: Taylor's paper never mentions photons, and his dim lamp wasn't a true single-photon source.

71
00:06:24,420 --> 00:06:29,060
>> So it was very faint light, not strictly one photon at a time.

72
00:06:29,140 --> 00:06:39,380
The real test came in 1986: a French team including Alain Aspect used a genuine single-photon source and an interferometer.

73
00:06:39,460 --> 00:06:43,880
>> And the interference was still there, one photon at a time.

74
00:06:43,960 --> 00:06:47,700
Here, each photon is detected at one single point.

75
00:06:47,780 --> 00:06:50,270
>> A few hundred look like noise.

76
00:06:50,350 --> 00:06:55,220
>> But by ten thousand, the stripes are unmistakable.

77
00:06:55,320 --> 00:06:57,240
>> And with brighter light?

78
00:06:57,320 --> 00:07:02,460
>> The dots just pile up faster: the same pattern, sooner.

79
00:07:05,060 --> 00:07:12,800
>> Electrons came the other way round: from 1897, experiments showed them behaving as particles.

80
00:07:12,880 --> 00:07:18,890
>> Then, in 1924, Louis de Broglie proposed that all matter has a wave side too.

81
00:07:18,970 --> 00:07:22,430
>> All matter? Electrons, atoms, everything?

82
00:07:22,510 --> 00:07:24,500
>> In principle, yes.

83
00:07:24,580 --> 00:07:32,790
>> And in 1927, experiments confirmed it for electrons, bouncing them off crystals and through thin films.

84
00:07:32,870 --> 00:07:38,910
>> But for decades, the two-slit version with electrons was taught only as a thought experiment.

85
00:07:38,990 --> 00:07:46,430
>> Richard Feynman even wrote, in his famous lectures: "This experiment has never been done in just this way."

86
00:07:46,510 --> 00:07:51,320
>> Except it had, in 1961, the year those lectures began.

87
00:07:51,400 --> 00:07:53,040
>> So who did it?

88
00:07:53,120 --> 00:07:59,960
>> Claus Jönsson, in Tübingen, made slits for electrons and saw the fringes, with up to five slits.

89
00:08:00,040 --> 00:08:06,130
>> His paper was in German, and didn't appear in English until 1974.

90
00:08:06,210 --> 00:08:13,300
That same year, in Bologna, Merli, Missiroli and Pozzi sent electrons through one at a time.

91
00:08:13,380 --> 00:08:19,950
>> Not through slits, though: they used an electron biprism, a fine wire that splits the beam in two.

92
00:08:20,030 --> 00:08:26,120
Then in 1989, Akira Tonomura and colleagues at Hitachi made it cleaner still.

93
00:08:26,200 --> 00:08:33,010
>> Fewer than 1,000 electrons arrived each second, so two were never in the apparatus at once.

94
00:08:33,090 --> 00:08:37,660
>> So there was nothing for an electron to interfere with, except itself.

95
00:08:37,740 --> 00:08:45,030
>> Each one arrived as a single flash: 8 electrons, then 270, then 2,000.

96
00:08:45,110 --> 00:08:51,300
>> And over a 20-minute exposure, 60,000 electrons drew the stripes.

97
00:08:51,380 --> 00:09:01,280
>> In 2002, Physics World readers voted the single-electron version the most beautiful experiment in physics.

98
00:09:03,880 --> 00:09:06,850
>> So how big can a thing be and still do this?

99
00:09:06,930 --> 00:09:13,340
>> In 1991, helium atoms interfered after passing through slits in gold foil.

100
00:09:13,420 --> 00:09:22,030
>> In 1999, a team in Vienna sent buckyballs, molecules of 60 carbon atoms, through a grating of many slits.

101
00:09:22,110 --> 00:09:29,670
>> A buckyball is 0.7 nanometres across, and its wave was some 280 times smaller.

102
00:09:29,750 --> 00:09:33,860
>> A wave far smaller than the molecule, and it still interfered?

103
00:09:33,940 --> 00:09:35,250
>> It did.

104
00:09:35,330 --> 00:09:44,250
>> And the record kept growing: molecules of 810 atoms in 2013, and about 2,000 atoms in 2019.

105
00:09:44,330 --> 00:09:52,740
>> Then in 2026, a team in Vienna showed interference with sodium clusters of more than 7,000 atoms each.

106
00:09:52,820 --> 00:09:56,260
>> Seven thousand atoms, behaving like a wave.

107
00:09:56,340 --> 00:10:02,030
>> The heavier the object, the shorter its wave, and the harder its stripes are to see.

108
00:10:02,110 --> 00:10:04,770
>> So is there a size where it simply stops?

109
00:10:04,850 --> 00:10:11,870
>> That's still debated, but so far quantum theory has passed every one of these tests.

110
00:10:14,470 --> 00:10:16,780
Now for the most famous twist.

111
00:10:16,860 --> 00:10:21,880
>> Put a detector at each slit, to find out which one each particle goes through.

112
00:10:21,960 --> 00:10:27,300
>> Every particle is found at one slit or the other, never split between them.

113
00:10:27,380 --> 00:10:29,870
>> And the stripes vanish.

114
00:10:29,950 --> 00:10:32,370
>> Gone, just from checking the path.

115
00:10:32,450 --> 00:10:39,090
>> The screen now shows two single-slit patterns, simply added together, with no interference.

116
00:10:39,170 --> 00:10:44,080
>> Why? To learn the path, the detector has to interact with the particle.

117
00:10:44,160 --> 00:10:49,820
>> And that leaves a record of which way it went, so the two paths can no longer interfere.

118
00:10:49,900 --> 00:10:53,220
>> Which is where the popular story goes wrong.

119
00:10:53,300 --> 00:11:00,840
It's often told as if a conscious observer changes the outcome, but the standard account needs no mind at all.

120
00:11:00,920 --> 00:11:03,060
>> So no one has to be watching?

121
00:11:03,140 --> 00:11:09,330
>> No: what matters is whether the path could be known, in principle, from anything left behind.

122
00:11:09,410 --> 00:11:18,450
>> In 2004, for example, a Vienna team laser-heated carbon-70 molecules, so they gave off heat radiation in flight.

123
00:11:18,530 --> 00:11:26,320
>> That radiation carried traces of each molecule's path, and the hotter the molecules, the fainter the stripes.

124
00:11:26,400 --> 00:11:29,990
>> And nobody looked at that light; it only had to exist.

125
00:11:30,070 --> 00:11:33,240
>> The molecules gave themselves away.

126
00:11:33,320 --> 00:11:41,280
>> And it isn't all or nothing: a 1987 experiment gathered some path information and kept fainter stripes.

127
00:11:41,360 --> 00:11:46,730
>> The better the two paths can be told apart, the fainter the stripes have to be.

128
00:11:46,810 --> 00:11:50,760
>> A trade-off, then, not an on-off switch.

129
00:11:53,360 --> 00:11:58,000
Richard Feynman put this experiment at the centre of quantum physics.

130
00:11:58,080 --> 00:12:04,400
>> He called it "impossible, absolutely impossible, to explain in any classical way."

131
00:12:04,480 --> 00:12:10,990
>> It "has in it the heart of quantum mechanics," he said. "In reality, it contains the only mystery."

132
00:12:11,070 --> 00:12:14,260
>> The only mystery. Strong words.

133
00:12:14,340 --> 00:12:21,330
>> So here's what quantum mechanics says: each particle is described by a wave that passes through both slits.

134
00:12:21,410 --> 00:12:28,000
>> The wave only sets the odds of where each particle lands: the square of the wave at each spot.

135
00:12:28,080 --> 00:12:33,690
>> Each detection is still a single dot, and only the pile of dots reveals the wave.

136
00:12:33,770 --> 00:12:37,140
>> But what is it doing between the slits and the screen?

137
00:12:37,220 --> 00:12:41,790
>> That's exactly where interpretations of quantum mechanics part ways.

138
00:12:41,870 --> 00:12:49,780
>> In one view, asking which slit means nothing without a detector; in others, there are many worlds, or a guiding wave.

139
00:12:49,860 --> 00:12:53,680
>> But they all predict the same stripes, dot for dot.

140
00:12:53,760 --> 00:12:59,370
So: Young's stripes showed light is a wave; single dots showed it arrives in lumps.

141
00:12:59,450 --> 00:13:04,990
>> Then electrons, atoms and molecules turned out to do exactly the same.

142
00:13:05,070 --> 00:13:09,610
>> And a record of the path, of any kind, wipes the stripes away.

143
00:13:09,690 --> 00:13:12,350
>> All from a wall with two slits in it.

144
00:13:12,430 --> 00:13:20,600
>> Two slits, a screen, and one dot at a time: it's still the clearest view we have of the quantum world.
